 1.3 **The KLR tower.** For the standard level-one cyclotomic quotient every block $R^\Lambda(\alpha)$ is finite dimensional and carries the homogeneous symmetrizing functional of [F5], so the shift-orbit bases of [F2] apply blockwise without any positivity hypothesis [F4, F5]. Since the root degree is additive, a finite combination of block classes has finite support and belongs to the restricted direct sum. [F2, F4, F5]
 
-1.4 **Transport to the Hecke tower.** For each residue-content block, the blockwise theorem of [F5] gives a graded $k$-algebra isomorphism $\varphi_\alpha:H^\Lambda_\alpha\to R^\Lambda(\alpha)$ matched with the chosen admissible normalising series, and the inherited grading of [F5] makes $\varphi_\alpha$ homogeneous. Restriction of scalars along the inverse of $\varphi_\alpha$ is an isomorphism of graded module categories: it is exact because it is induced by an algebra isomorphism, it preserves finite-dimensional modules, and it preserves projectives because restriction along an isomorphism has the exact quasi-inverse of extension along $\varphi_\alpha^{-1}$, which is also exact. Hence it induces group isomorphisms $K_0^{\mathrm{gr}}(H^\Lambda)\cong K_0^{\mathrm{gr}}(R^\Lambda)$ and $G_0^{\mathrm{gr}}(H^\Lambda)\cong G_0^{\mathrm{gr}}(R^\Lambda)$; these are $A$-linear because a homogeneous isomorphism carries $M\{1\}$ to $M\{1\}$, and they intertwine the Cartan maps and the forgetful maps $\zeta$ with the ungraded identifications. [F1, F3, F5]
